course: dictionary
lesson: 638
title: Backstage
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Backstage is a word you will meet in plays, concerts and stories. It is made of two parts, back and stage. Put them together and you get the area behind the stage, where performers wait and get ready.
explain: «The stage crew work backstage, often dressed in black so the audience does not notice them.» The clues are stage crew, dressed in black and the audience does not notice them. So backstage must be a hidden place, out of sight of the people watching.
explain: Backstage can be an adverb (go backstage), an adjective (a backstage pass) or a noun. Read the words around it to decide which meaning fits. In a story, it can also mean in secret, behind the scenes.
fact: Backstage means in the area behind the stage of a theatre, where performers get ready and wait.
fact: Backstage can also mean in private, hidden from the public, where the real planning happens.
fact: «The stage crew work backstage» means they work behind the stage, out of the audience's view.
fact: A «backstage pass» lets fans meet the performers.
fact: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence: «The stage crew work behind the stage» still makes sense.
exercise: «The stage crew work backstage, often dressed in black so the audience does not notice them.» Where do the stage crew work? | Outside in the car park | In the front row with the audience | *Behind the stage, out of sight | On the stage, in the spotlight
exercise: «A backstage pass lets fans meet the performers.» What does a backstage pass do? | Lets you sing on the stage | *Lets you meet the performers | Lets you park your car near the theatre | Lets you sit in the front row
exercise: «The stage crew work backstage, often dressed in black so the audience does not notice them.» Find and copy one word from the sentence that means the people watching a show. | *audience | dressed | black | stage
test: In a school play, where do pupils wait and change their costumes? | *Backstage | In the audience | Outside the school gate | In the head teacher's office
test: What does «backstage» mean in a story about a secret plan made backstage? | In a car park | Loudly, on the stage | Very happy and excited | *In secret, hidden from view
exam: Which sentence uses backstage as an adjective? | *Fans waved their backstage passes | The crew worked backstage all night | Please go backstage now | We sat backstage in the stalls
